          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Traditional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            is the field of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            that studies the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+spaces"} smooth spaces
            which are equipped with a notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiation"} differentiation
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
          %p
            In higher differential geometry,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            is paired with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+spaces"} smooth spaces
            are refined to “smooth
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoids"} smooth infinity-groupoids
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-stacks"} infinity-stacks
            – and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+algebras"} function algebras
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            are refined to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+homotopy+algebras"} strong homotopy algebras
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-algebra+over+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-operad"} infinity-algebras
          %p Here we give an informal overview of what motivates this generalization and what it is good for.
          %p
            First we list some examples of how tools from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
            help to understand plain traditional
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#ApplicationsOfHigherDGToPlainDG"} I) Applications of higher DG to plain DG
          %p
            Besides illuminating traditional DG where it exists, higher DG naturally extends plain DG to regions where it breaks down due to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singularities"} singularities
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#ResolutionOfSingularities"} II) Resolution of singularities in plain DG
          %p
            However, much of the genuine motivation for developing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
            came and increasingly comes from structures in fundamental
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometric
            nature, hence for which no adequate description in plain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            even exists (this is in fact already true for traditional local gauge theory, see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#FieldBundles"} below
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#MotivationOfHigherDGFromPhysics"} III) Motivation of higher DG from physics
          %h2#ApplicationsOfHigherDGToPlainDG
            %strong I)
            Applications of higher DG to plain DG
          %p
            This section lists examples of how
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
            helps with understanding plain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#FoliationsAndPDEs"} Foliations and PDEs
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#PoissonGeometry"} Poisson and symplectic geometry
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#DeformationTheory"} Deformation theory
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#Orbifolds"} Orbifolds
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#FieldBundles"} Field bundles for local gauge theory
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#LieIntegration"} Lie integration
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#VariationalCalculusAndSymplecticReduction"} Variational calculus and symplectic reduction
          %h3#FoliationsAndPDEs Foliations and PDEs
          %p
            At the roots of differential geometry, prominent in the original work of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eli+Cartan"} Eli Cartan
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foliation"} foliation
            theory, arising in the integration of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+differential+equations"} partial differential equations
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoid
            theory, foliations have a classification – a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haefliger+groupoid"} Haefliger groupoid
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#MoerdijkMrcun"} Moerdijk-Mrcun
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haefliger+theorem"} Haefliger theorem
            which classifies foliations on open manifolds is proven by way of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haefliger+groupoid"} Haefliger groupoid
          %p
            Cartan had originally encoded the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+differential+equation"} PDEs
            whose integration leads to foliation by “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exterior+differential+systems"}> exterior differential systems
            ”. These are equivalently (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebras"}> Chevalley-Eilenberg algebras
            ) of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E+algebroids"} L-∞ algebroids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory"} Lie theory
            –
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-Lie+theory"} infinity-Lie theory
          %h3#PoissonGeometry Poisson and symplectic geometry
          %p
            A subtopic of this is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+geometry"} Poisson geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+leaves"} symplectic leaves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifolds"} Poisson manifolds
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            problem was solved by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kontsevich"} Kontsevich
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cattaneo"} Cattaneo
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Felder"} Felder
            then showed that his formula for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
            is naturally understood as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-point+function"} 3-point function
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+sigma+model"} Poisson sigma model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroid"} Poisson Lie algebroid
            of the given Poisson manifold.
          %p
            The strengthening of this to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+deformation+quantization"} strict deformation quantization
            problem for a large class of cases was then established by forming the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convolution+algebra"} convolution algebra
            of sections of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+bundle"} prequantization
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integrates
            the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroid"} Poisson Lie algebroid
          %p
            (In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
            the shift into
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            witnessed in both these proofs is a special case of what is known as the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic principle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroids"} Poisson Lie algebroids
            and
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoids"} symplectic groupoids
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
            that induces the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+deformation+quantization"} strict deformation quantization
            of a Poisson manifold also solves its classical desingularization problem, its
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+realization"} symplectic realization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            with base a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            the target map is a symplectic realization of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            and the source map is a symplectic realization of the opposite structure. Thus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            with its symplectic structure may be regarded as a desingularization of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            with its Poisson structure. Since the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroid"} Poisson Lie algebroid
            of the Poisson manifold, symplectic realization is reduced to a problem in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory"} Lie theory
          %h3#DeformationTheory Deformation theory
          %p
            Apart from these Poisson structures and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+structures"} symplectic structures
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+structures"} complex structures
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+geometry"} complex geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+space"} infinitesimally
            given by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+theory"} deformation theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+for+L-%E2%88%9E+algebras"} model structure for L-∞ algebras
            it is known that deformation theory is essentially equivalently the study of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} L-∞ algebra
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+theory"} ∞-Lie theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/period+maps"} period maps
            find their natural formulation in this context. This is amplified in (
            %a{:href => "#FiorenzaMartinengo"}> Fiorenza-Martinengo2012
            ).
          %h3#Orbifolds Orbifolds
          %p
            Almost as basic to traditional differential geometry as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds"} manifolds
            are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/actions"} actions
            into the theory. Notably the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+spaces"} moduli spaces
            arising in differential geometry tend to be orbifolds instead of manifolds.
          %p
            But orbifolds are equivalently a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+Lie+groupoid"} proper
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+Lie+groupoid"} étale Lie groupoid
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne-Mumford+stacks"} Deligne-Mumford stacks
          %h3#FieldBundles Field bundles for local gauge theory
          %p
            Differential geometry shares much of its origin and history with
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+fields"} tensor fields
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+geometry"} Riemannian geometry
            grew out of the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Weil+theory"} Chern-Weil theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            out of the study of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxwell%27s+equations"} Maxwell's equations
            influenced the study of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham cohomology
          %p
            Out of this tradition has grown the formalization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+fields"} physical fields
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
            over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
            %em both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field+theory"} gauge field theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+quantum+field+theory"} local quantum field theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
          %p
            The reason is that a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton+sector"} instanton sector
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charge"} charge
            sector, is not a section of a fiber bundle but of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+infinity-bundle"} fiber 2-bundle
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field (physics)
            and in the corresponding section at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics"} geometry of physics
          %h3#LieIntegration Lie integration
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            of anything more general than a finite-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            in general goes out of the real of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groups"} Lie groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebroids"} Lie algebroids
            integrate to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoids"} Lie groupoids
            and
          %p
            infinite-dimensional Lie algebras in general to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+2-groups"} Lie 2-groups
          %p (…)
          %h3#VariationalCalculusAndSymplecticReduction Variational calculus and symplectic reduction
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+differential+geometry"} derived differential geometry
            allows to resolve many otherwise singular limit/intersection constructions. Modern
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+calculus"} variational calculus
            in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundle"} jet bundle
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-geometry"}> D-geometry
            ”) is formulated this way, as is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+reduction"} symplectic reduction
            and the unification of both in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+formalism"} BV-BRST formalism
          %h2#ResolutionOfSingularities
            %strong II)
            Resolution of singularities in plain DG
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singularity"} Singularities
            arise in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            if the two kinds of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+constructions"} universal constructions
            fail to exist as
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                if an
                %em intersection
                (in
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/critical+loci"} critical loci
                to
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+critical+loci"} derived critical loci
              %p
                %a{:href => "#ResolutionOfSingularIntersections"} Resolution of singular intersection
            %li
              %p
                if a
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
                (in
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+quotients"} homotopy quotients
              %p
                %a{:href => "#ResolutionOfSingularQuotients"} Resolution of singular quotients: stacks quotients
          %h3#ResolutionOfSingularQuotients Resolution of singular qotients: stacky quotients
          %p
            …
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            …
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+quotient"} homotopy quotient
            …
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+stack"} quotient stack
            …
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+stack"} differentiable stack
            …
          %h3#ResolutionOfSingularIntersections Resolution of singular intersections: derived intersections
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            does not have many
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/critical+locus"} critical locus
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            – of central relevance in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+calculus"} variational calculus
            – rarely is again a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            and hence cannot be treated with tools of differential geometry, in general.
          %p
            In the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+differential+geometry"}> derived differential geometry
            ”-flavor of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
            (technically: the not-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-localic+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"}> 1-localic
            ) this problem is resolved by replacig
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+algebras"} function algebras
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+algebras"} simplicial algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-manifolds"} dg-manifolds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resolution"} resolution
            familiar from
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology+group"} homology group
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
            of perfectly smooth manifolds (or rather their function algebras).
          %p
            Applied the concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/critical+locus"} critical locus
            this leads to the notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+critical+locus"} derived critical locus
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+formalism"} BV-BRST formalism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+differential+geometry"} derived differential geometry
            provide a theoretical framework for handling
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+formalism"} BV-BRST formalism
            systematically.
          %h2#MotivationOfHigherDGFromPhysics
            %strong III)
            Motivation of higher DG from physics
          %p
            To a large extent,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            had been co-evolving with the description of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            in terms of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            %em
              %a{:href => "#FieldBundles"} Field bundles for local gauge theory
            that an accurate formulation even of traditional notions of such fields requires
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
            to degree 1. But modern developments in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            require notions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+fields"} physical fields
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            that probe much more deeply into the realm of geometric
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics"} geometry of physics
              \.
          %p
            A more technical survey of is in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#FSS"} FSS 13
          %p
            Below we first discuss how combining the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+quantum+field+theory"} local quantum field theory
            in its modern incarnation as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+quantum+field+theory"} extended quantum field theory
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"} Lagrangian
            quantum field theory necessitates higher differential geometric structures:
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#ExtendedPrequantumFieldTheory"} Extended prequantum field theory
          %p
            Then we turn to modern proposals for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theories
            that go beyond the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            and which inherently contain higher geometric fields already in their non-localized formalization:
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#HigherGaugeFields"} Higher gauge fields
          %p (…)
          %h3#ExtendedPrequantumFieldTheory The higher orbit method – Extended prequantum field theory
          %p
            A high point of traditional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            is its impact on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
            via the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbit+method"} orbit method
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            equipped with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+action"} Hamiltonian action
            by a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mechanical+system"} mechanical system
            and then applying the differential-geometric process of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            to its yields a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+states"} space of states
            on which the group is represented. A large class of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representations"} representations
            arises this way and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbit+method"} orbit method
            sheds light on the corresponding
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
          %p
            But speaking of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            in the first place, certainly the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            governing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbit+method"} orbit method
            is just the simplest fragment of the foundational theory of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            in arbitrary
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            = succeed "\u201D)." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbit+method"} orbit method
            from dimension 1 to higher dimensional quantum field theory corresponds precisely, as we discuss now, to passing to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+prequantum+field+theory"} extended prequantum geometry
          %p
            The notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            exists without reference to any predefined notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space"} configuration space
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+fields"} quantum fields
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            etc.:
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            in
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiomatization"} axiomatization
            is simply a consistent assignment of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+quantum+states"} spaces of quantum states
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiomatization"} axiomatization
            it is a consistent assignment of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+quantum+observables"} algebras of quantum observables
          %p
            However, most (or maybe all?) quantum field theories of interest in actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            (as opposed to as devices of pure
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            from a (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangian"}> local
            \/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+Lagrangian"}> extended
            )
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space"} configuration space
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+fields"} quantum fields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+duals"} holographic duals
            of quantum field theories that arise by quantization. Moreover, the extra information provided by the Lagrangian is commonly used (and is maybe strictly necessary) to interpret the mathematical structure of the axiomatic QFT in actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            (though notably in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            there are results that re-extract at least parts of this data from the axiomatic QFT, for instance the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Doplicher-Roberts+reconstruction+theorem"} Doplicher-Roberts reconstruction theorem
            which extract the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+gauge+group"} global gauge group
            from the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net+of+quantum+observables"} local net of quantum observables
          %p
            There are in turn two formalizations of the notion of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            %em
              algebraic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantization"} prequantization
            when referring to a precursor step to the actual quantization step, in which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            is lifted from to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+bundle"} prequantum bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometric+quantization"} higher geometric quantization
            this prequantization step is already part of the data of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            itself: an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+Lagrangian"} extended Lagrangian
            already encodes not just the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            but also the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+bundle"} prequantum bundle
            and all the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+n-bundle"} prequantum (n-k)-bundles
            in each
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+0-bundle"} prequantum 0-bundle
            in this context.
          %p
            Therefore, in the refined picture of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+quantum+field+theory"} extended quantum field theory
            it makes good sense to refer in a unified way to
            %strong prequantum field theory
            for all of the data related to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangians"} Lagrangians
            that is not yet the final
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
          %p
            In particular, an
            %strong extended prequantum field theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            is a rule that assigns
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                to every (suitably
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} oriented
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifold"} closed manifold
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+n-bundle"} prequantum (n-k)-bundle
            %li
              %p
                to every (suitably
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} oriented
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold+with+boundary"} manifold with boundary
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_k
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
                of the prequantum
                = succeed "-bundle" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mi k
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-k+1)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial \Sigma_k
                and pulled back to the space of fields over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_k
          %p
            such that this data is related suitably under
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transgression"} transgression
          %p
            The actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+quantum+field+theory"} extended quantum field theory
            would be obtained from such a data by passing from the assignment of a given prequantum
            = succeed "-bundle" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-k)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-vector+space"} (n-k)-vector space
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"} polarized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
            of a suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+infinity-bundle"} associated
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+infinity-bundle"} fiber bundle
          %p This is summarized in the following table:
          %div
            %p
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+prequantum+field+theory"} extended prequantum field theory
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 0
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi k
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi n
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \leq k \leq n
                  %th
                    (off-shell)
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+n-bundle"} prequantum (n-k)-bundle
                  %th traditional terminology
              %tbody
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 0
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+characteristic+map"} universal characteristic map
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/level+%28Chern-Simons+theory%29"} level
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+n-bundle"} prequantum (n-1)-bundle
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-Wess-Zumino-Witten+model"} WZW bundle (n-2)-gerbe
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi k
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+n-bundle"} prequantum (n-k)-bundle
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n-1
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+n-bundle"} prequantum 1-bundle
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    (off-shell)
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+bundle"} prequantum bundle
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+0-bundle"} prequantum 0-bundle
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
          %h3#HigherGaugeFields Higher gauge fields
          %p
            Beyond the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+n-bundles"} prequantum n-bundles
            involved in any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+prequantum+field+theory"} extended prequantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theories
            in physics that involve
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            which themselves are already given by higher geometric structure, notably
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+fields"} higher gauge fields
            given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+an+infinity-bundle"} higher connections
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+infinity-bundle"} higher principal bundles
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theories
            and their
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-completion"} UV-completion
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
          %p
            The first example along these lines is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly"} quantum anomaly
            cancellation conditions can lead to fairly intricate structures of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+infinity-stacks"} moduli infinity-stacks
            of such fields. For instance anomaly-free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+fields"} background gauge fields
            are given by moduli 2-stacks of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+differential+string+structures"} twisted differential string structures
          %div
            %p
              %strong
                Table of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane"} branes
                appearing in
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
              (for classification see at
              = succeed ")." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane+scan"} brane scan
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane"} brane
                  %th
                    in
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
                  %th
